msgid "Pin a version"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Fix a citable version of the study.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A study changes for years, while a paper, a report or a delivery cites "
"the study as it was on a given day. **Pin version…** writes that state as"
" a separate file which later work cannot reach: the stable thing a "
"citation, and a DOI, can point at."
msgstr ""

msgid "Read the version of the study"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When the study has a version, the status bar shows **Project** followed "
"by the version number, for example *Project v12*. Hold the pointer on it "
"to read:"
msgstr ""

msgid "the **content digest**, a fingerprint computed from the graphs;"
msgstr ""

msgid "**revision of**, the fingerprint of the version it came from;"
msgstr ""

msgid "**modified**, the date and time of the version."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The version advances only when the content changes: when the file is "
"saved or pinned after a change to its graphs, and when it receives the "
"graphs of another file (see :doc:`integrate-another-file`). Saving a file"
" whose graphs have not changed leaves the number as it is, and so does a "
"change that is only a way of looking at the graphs, such as a colour "
"scheme (see :doc:`clean-the-file`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Pin"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Save the study first, so that the pinned version is the one on the disk "
"(see :doc:`open-save-recent`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click a graph of the file you want to pin: EMStudio pins the file of the "
"active graph."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose ``File ▸ Pin version…``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio settles the version and hands the snapshot over as a download, "
"named after the graph with the version number and the ending "
"``.pinned.em.json``, for example ``Temple-v12.pinned.em.json``. The file "
"holds the whole study as it is, with its version, its fingerprint and the"
" date and time of the pinning. The message **pinned** repeats the version"
" and its fingerprint, and the Log keeps the same line."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the study has no graph yet, a message says that there is nothing to "
"pin."
msgstr ""

msgid "Keep the pinned file"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Move the pinned file into the folder of the project, beside the study, or"
" into the archive where your citable data lives. Do not edit it: its "
"value is to stay as it was written. Continue your work in the "
"``.em.json`` of the study, whose version will advance with the next "
"changes."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The DOI is minted by the Catalog; the pinned file is the stable thing it "
"points at."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A snapshot of the study with a version number and a fingerprint, which a "
"citation can name and anyone can check against the content."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/reference/emjson`, the version numbers of an ``.em.json`` and the "
"version of the work."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/how-to/archive/versions-for-each-use`, the versions of the models "
"for each use, a different thing from the version of the study."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`project-folder`, where to keep the study and its files."
msgstr ""

